<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>
  <TerrainDef Name="RL_EmptyBase" Abstract="True">
    <passability>Impassable</passability>
    <pathCost>300</pathCost>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <edgeType>Hard</edgeType>
    <renderPrecedence>300</renderPrecedence>
    <layerable>false</layerable>
    <changeable>false</changeable>
    <holdSnowOrSand>false</holdSnowOrSand>
    <avoidWander>true</avoidWander>
    <preventCraters>true</preventCraters>
    <canEverTerraform>false</canEverTerraform>
    <supportsRock>false</supportsRock>
    <canBePolluted>false</canBePolluted>
    <takeFootprints>false</takeFootprints>
    <filthAcceptanceMask><li>None</li></filthAcceptanceMask>
    <statBases><WorkToBuild>0</WorkToBuild><Flammability>0</Flammability><MarketValue>0</MarketValue></statBases>
    <affordances />
  </TerrainDef>
  <TerrainDef ParentName="RL_EmptyBase">
    <defName>RL_OpenAir</defName>
    <dontRender>true</dontRender>
    <changeable>true</changeable>
    <!--
      Open air has to answer the same terrain questions the vacuum does. Every SOS2 hull part is a
      plain Building whose parent template asks for Light/Medium/Heavy, and SOS2's space map is filled
      with EmptySpace, which grants all three. An upper band filled with a terrain that grants none of
      them makes the whole deck read as "wrong terrain" to parts that place fine on the ground band -
      hardpoints, engines and every other BuildingBase child.

      Granting them is safe because they only decide what may be placed. Whether a cell has a floor
      is StructuralSupport.HasSurface, which rejects RL_OpenAir by name, so a building still cannot
      stand in open air without a deck under it.
    -->
    <affordances><li>Light</li><li>Medium</li><li>Heavy</li><li>RL_FloorSupport</li></affordances>
    <label>open air</label>
    <description>Empty space on an upper layer. It is not a walkable floor.</description>
  </TerrainDef>
  <!--
    The same role as RL_OpenAir, but it is the def a band gets when the map it belongs to is a space
    map. Ground, underground and Odyssey gravship maps keep RL_OpenAir and never see this def.

    It is dontRender for the same reason RL_OpenAir is, and that reason is not cosmetic. A band is a
    range of cells in the same map, so painting it paints over whatever the renderer would otherwise
    show at that screen position: the band below and the map's own background. An empty band that
    draws an opaque texture therefore hides the deck underneath it and the starfield behind it, which
    is exactly what happens if this def gets a texture.

    The vacuum look a space map has comes from the map background, not from the empty bands standing
    in front of it. Leaving the band unseen is what lets that background show through.

    So the def is about identity, not appearance: it is what tells the player this is the space above
    an orbiting hull rather than the space above a basement, it carries the Space tag and it
    extinguishes fire. StructuralSupport.IsOpenAir accepts both defs, so nothing that asks "is this
    empty air?" has to know which map it is on.
  -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="RL_EmptyBase">
    <defName>RL_SpaceAir</defName>
    <dontRender>true</dontRender>
    <renderPrecedence>20</renderPrecedence>
    <pathCost>50</pathCost>
    <changeable>true</changeable>
    <tags><li>Space</li></tags>
    <extinguishesFire>true</extinguishesFire>
    <affordances><li>Light</li><li>Medium</li><li>Heavy</li><li>RL_FloorSupport</li></affordances>
    <label>space</label>
    <description>Empty space on an upper layer of a ship in orbit. It is not a walkable floor.</description>
  </TerrainDef>
  <TerrainDef ParentName="RL_EmptyBase">
    <defName>RL_Isolation</defName>
    <dontRender>true</dontRender>
    <label>layer isolation</label>
    <description>Reserved storage space between logical layers. It has no physical location.</description>
  </TerrainDef>
</Defs>
